From 02eb8de9e0155b58de8cee4b188d9c40cea0c562 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:10:50 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 20/60] Let a family's round planner drive the lane host with
 prompt pieces

A family can now hand the lane host a round planner (api.Rounds) that admits
requests, splits prompts into pieces and runs them in the same rounds as the
other streams' decode rows. The planner is opt-in: the host's own loop, with a
backend's prefill_step a chunk a loop between rounds, stays the default, and a
host with a planner runs the planner's loop instead (LaneHost.run picks one).
The backend gains optional entries for this: begin, piece, pieces (several
streams' pieces in one call), finish and join, so a round's finished prompts
get their first drafts in one backend call.

A planner's prompts open through lanes/open.zig like the whole and the chunked
pass (open.openedWith; their first drafts sized by the head's reach under node
probabilities), and a prompt cancelled at its final releases its lane as a
cancelled chunk does.

The lane core also takes a family row policy (lanes/trim.zig) in place of the
built-in allocation, proposers that price their own drafts, and a timed switch
for backends without round timers. Under a planner the host still steps the
learner while no request waits or runs, and a failed piece or piece run fails
only its own requests.

A planner over a backend without prompt pieces (such as the external drafter
wrapper) is refused at start with error.PiecesUnsupported.

The round loop lives in lane_rounds.zig, a request's stream setup in
lane_stream.zig and the pieces' steps in lanes/pieces.zig, keeping the host
and the engine under the line limit.

(cherry picked from commit 0918809379cadc6ad118ab2ab780e7641b0ae2b6)
